WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.510
In sixteen fourteen, John Napier

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.060
published a book of tables that took him
about twenty years to compute by hand.

3
00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.700
They rested on one idea,

4
00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:15.630
and that idea let astronomers turn every
long multiplication into an addition,

5
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.990
which is far quicker
and far harder to get wrong.

6
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.950
He called the numbers
in those tables logarithms.

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.790
That idea turned out to be much larger
than a shortcut for astronomers.

8
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.980
It counts how many times you multiply,
it turns a ruler into a calculator,

9
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.890
and its graph is the mirror
image of exponential growth.

10
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.610
It hides in the area under a simple curve,
which is where the number e comes from,

11
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.260
and it is the scale our ears
use to hear the world.

12
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.150
It even lets us draw an atom
and a galaxy on the same page.

13
00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.440
You may have met logarithms
as a button on a calculator,

14
00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.210
or as rules to memorize before an exam.

15
00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.530
In this video we will build them step
by step, work through examples together,

16
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.870
and see where every rule comes from.

17
00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:11.490
And we will start with a single question:
how many times do you have to multiply?

18
00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.810
Take a single cell and let it divide.

19
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:21.620
After one division there are two cells,
after another there are four, then eight,

20
00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.560
then sixteen.

21
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.630
Each division doubles the count,
so after only ten divisions there

22
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.080
are one thousand and twenty four cells.

23
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.530
Now turn the question around.

24
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:37.720
If a dish holds one thousand and twenty
four cells, how many divisions made them?

25
00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.330
The answer is ten,
and that number is a logarithm.

26
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.440
We can draw this as a staircase.

27
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.610
Each step up is one more multiplication
by two, so the height of the staircase

28
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.360
counts the steps.

29
00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.480
Ten twos multiplied together give
one thousand and twenty four,

30
00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.610
and we write that the logarithm, base two,
of one thousand and twenty four is ten.

31
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.500
Both lines state the same fact,

32
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:08.350
one read from the number of steps
and the other from the result.

33
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.940
The base is the size of each step.

34
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.530
With base ten,

35
00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.840
each step multiplies by ten, so ten,

36
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.800
one hundred and one thousand sit one,

37
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.940
two and three steps up,
and the logarithm counts the zeros.

38
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.070
Try one yourself.

39
00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.000
How many threes do you multiply
together to reach eighty one?

40
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.740
Three times three is nine,
then twenty seven, then eighty one,

41
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.357
so it takes four threes,
and the logarithm, base three,

42
00:02:39.407 --> 00:02:41.200
of eighty one is four.

43
00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.930
The staircase also has
room between its steps.

44
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.940
Six lies between four and eight,
so its logarithm, base two,

45
00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.720
lies between two and three,
at about two point five eight.

46
00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:58.550
Fill in every number like this,
and the staircase smooths into a curve.

47
00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.500
Napier's trick comes straight
out of the staircase.

48
00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:06.620
Write the powers of two in one row,

49
00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.400
and the number of steps
for each of them underneath.

50
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:14.940
To multiply eight by thirty two,
look up their steps, three and five.

51
00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.600
Add them to get eight,
and read across the table:

52
00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:22.700
eight steps up is two hundred
and fifty six, which is the product.

53
00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.627
Climbing three steps and then five
is the same as climbing eight,

54
00:03:27.653 --> 00:03:30.260
so the multiplication
has become an addition.

55
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:36.670
That is the main rule: the logarithm
of a product is the sum of the logarithms.

56
00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.930
The other rules follow
from the same picture.

57
00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.040
Dividing walks back down the staircase,

58
00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.100
so the logarithm
of a quotient is a difference.

59
00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:50.900
Raising a number to a power repeats
the same climb several times,

60
00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.810
so the logarithm of a power
is that many copies of the climb.

61
00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.090
Every rule you may have memorized

62
00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.660
is the staircase seen
from a different side.

63
00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.890
These rules turn hard
questions into easy ones.

64
00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:05.890
How many digits does two

65
00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.510
to the hundredth power have,
a hundred twos multiplied together?

66
00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:15.260
Nobody wants to multiply that out,
but the power rule says its logarithm,

67
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.710
base ten,
is a hundred times the logarithm of two.

68
00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:20.600
The logarithm of two

69
00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.290
is about zero point three zero one,
so the answer is about thirty point one.

70
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.880
That puts the number between ten
to the thirty and ten to the thirty one,

71
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.010
so it has thirty one digits.

72
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.360
This is the kind of work Napier's
twenty years made possible.

73
00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:43.180
Around sixteen twenty, logarithms
were engraved onto a wooden rule.

74
00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:46.120
On this scale,
each number sits at a distance

75
00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.320
from the start equal to its logarithm.

76
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.870
One is at the very beginning,
ten is at the far end, and two sits

77
00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:59.293
at zero point three zero one of the way
along, the same number we just used.

78
00:04:59.547 --> 00:05:02.100
The marks crowd together
as the numbers grow.

79
00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:06.620
Put two of these scales side by side,
and you have a slide rule.

80
00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.540
To multiply two by three,

81
00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:14.360
slide the upper scale until its one
sits above the two on the lower scale.

82
00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.360
Then find three on the upper scale,

83
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.080
and read the number beneath it,
which is six.

84
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.420
The two lengths have
been added end to end,

85
00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:27.880
and because each length is a logarithm,
adding them multiplies the numbers.

86
00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.210
Engineers used slide rules like

87
00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:35.170
this for centuries, to design bridges,
aircraft and the Apollo rockets.

88
00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.940
Going from one to ten takes the same
distance as going from ten to a hundred,

89
00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.930
or from a hundred to a thousand, because
each of those is the same multiplication.

90
00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:51.050
On a piano, every octave doubles
the frequency of the note, yet each

91
00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.760
octave takes up the same width of keys.

92
00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.670
The keyboard is laid out on a logarithmic
scale, because our own ears hear this way.

93
00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:06.710
Now draw the curve of two to the x, which
doubles with every step to the right.

94
00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.930
The logarithm undoes that curve.

95
00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.940
Where the exponential
takes three to eight,

96
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:18.230
the logarithm takes eight back to three,
so its graph is the same curve reflected

97
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:19.990
across the diagonal line.

98
00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.307
Every point swaps its two coordinates,

99
00:06:23.333 --> 00:06:26.610
and the steep climb becomes a long,
slow rise.

100
00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.820
That rise is very slow.

101
00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:35.260
Out at a million, the logarithm,
base ten, has only reached six,

102
00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.710
and at a billion it has reached nine.

103
00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:42.510
So the common logarithm of a whole
number tells you, to within one,

104
00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.330
how many digits it has.

105
00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:49.190
The curve never stops climbing,
but it trades enormous distances

106
00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.190
for small, steady heights.

107
00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.530
Logarithms with different
bases all share one shape.

108
00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:58.030
The curve for base two is the curve

109
00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.510
for base ten stretched upward,
by the same factor at every point.

110
00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:04.977
So to change base,

111
00:07:05.027 --> 00:07:08.780
you divide by a single number,
the logarithm of the new base.

112
00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.210
This is how you solve
for an unknown exponent.

113
00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.980
Which power of two gives ten?

114
00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:18.670
The answer is the logarithm of ten,

115
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.460
base two, which is one divided
by the logarithm of two,

116
00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:26.140
about three point three two,
the same stretch we just saw.

117
00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.470
Mathematicians prefer one
base above all the others.

118
00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.790
It is a number close to two
point seven one eight, called e.

119
00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:38.430
To see where it comes from,
we need a piece of geometry.

120
00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:44.337
Take the curve whose height
at each point is one over x,

121
00:07:44.387 --> 00:07:47.190
and shade the area beneath it,
starting from one.

122
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.380
In the sixteen forties,

123
00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:53.320
mathematicians studying this curve
noticed something about those areas.

124
00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:55.660
The area from one to two,

125
00:07:55.710 --> 00:08:00.120
added to the area from one to three,
gives the area from one to six.

126
00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:05.220
Check the numbers: about zero point
six nine and one point one zero,

127
00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.950
which together make one point seven nine.

128
00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.590
Areas that add when their endpoints
multiply behave exactly like logarithms.

129
00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.390
There is a simple reason for this.

130
00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.310
Take the region from one
to two and stretch

131
00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.920
it sideways by a factor of three,
so it now runs from three to six.

132
00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.090
It becomes three times wider, but out
there the curve is three times lower,

133
00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:31.210
so the area does not change.

134
00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.330
So the area from three to six matches

135
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.420
the area from one to two, and together
with one to three it makes one to six.

136
00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:45.470
The area under this curve is a logarithm,
and the curve itself chooses its base.

137
00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:51.840
To find that base, slide the right edge
outward until the area is exactly one.

138
00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:57.850
The edge stops at two point seven one
eight two eight and so on, the number e.

139
00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.920
This area is the natural logarithm,

140
00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.870
written l n, and its slope at every
point is the height of the first curve.

141
00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:11.380
That is why e appears wherever something
grows at a rate set by its own size.

142
00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:15.770
Suppose an amount grows continuously
by seven percent a year.

143
00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.770
How long until it doubles?

144
00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:23.470
It doubles once the rate times the time
reaches the natural logarithm of two,

145
00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:25.620
about zero point six nine.

146
00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.760
So the doubling time is zero point six
nine divided by zero point zero seven,

147
00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:34.780
close to ten years, and that is where
the rule of seventy comes from.

148
00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:39.040
Our senses work the same way.

149
00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:43.850
We notice changes in proportion,
so a sound must grow by a fixed

150
00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:46.670
factor before it seems one step louder.

151
00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.930
That is why loudness is measured
in decibels, where every ten

152
00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:53.480
decibels means ten times the power.

153
00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.340
So how much more powerful
is a ninety decibel concert

154
00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.450
than a sixty decibel conversation?

155
00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.740
The difference is thirty decibels,
which is three steps of ten,

156
00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.880
so the concert carries
a thousand times the power.

157
00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:10.620
Star brightness works the same way,

158
00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:13.680
where five magnitudes mean
a hundred times the light.

159
00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:17.430
The same idea measures
the ground and the water.

160
00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:21.970
On the earthquake magnitude scale,
each whole step means about thirty

161
00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:24.120
two times more energy.

162
00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:28.690
Two steps multiply that by itself,
so a magnitude seven releases

163
00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.270
about a thousand times more
energy than a magnitude five.

164
00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:38.100
Acidity is measured by pH, which
counts powers of ten of hydrogen ions.

165
00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:40.740
So lemon juice, at about two,

166
00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:45.740
is roughly a hundred thousand times
more acidic than pure water at seven.

167
00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:49.440
Both scales lay a huge
range out in even steps.

168
00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:53.150
Logarithms also reveal hidden laws.

169
00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.870
Johannes Kepler found a rule linking

170
00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.880
each planet's distance from the Sun
to the time it takes to go around.

171
00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:01.740
Plotted plainly,

172
00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:06.500
the planets lie on a bending curve,
but take the logarithm of both quantities

173
00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:10.710
and they fall on a straight line,
rising three steps for every two.

174
00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:14.380
That straight line
is the mark of a power law.

175
00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:20.690
And with logarithmic steps, an atom,
a person, the Earth and a whole galaxy

176
00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:25.290
can share one ruler, about thirty
one powers of ten from end to end.

177
00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.670
So a logarithm is a count
of multiplications,

178
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.010
the number of steps up a staircase.

179
00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:37.810
Because steps add when numbers multiply,
products become sums,

180
00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:42.140
unknown exponents can be solved,
and a slide rule can multiply.

181
00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.940
Its graph is the exponential
seen in a mirror.

182
00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:48.550
The area under one over x gives

183
00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:53.120
the natural logarithm, and the point
where that area reaches one is e.

184
00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:58.243
And because we sense the world in ratios,
logarithms measure sound,

185
00:11:58.293 --> 00:12:01.750
starlight and earthquakes,
from atoms to galaxies.

186
00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.320
Napier spent twenty years

187
00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:08.510
on his tables so that other people
could spend less time on arithmetic.

188
00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:12.950
Four centuries later,
his idea is still doing that work.

189
00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.000
So the next time you see a logarithm,

190
00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.070
read it as a quiet question:
how many times did we multiply?

191
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.400
Thanks for watching.
